At each bit position, we first subtract the divisor from the left half of the remainder

232
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:50,920
register.

233
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:57,080
Since the resulting remainder is not positive, then we perform an addition to restore.

234
00:17:57,080 --> 00:18:05,640
Finally, we shift into zero for the quotient since the divisor did not fit.

235
00:18:05,640 --> 00:18:09,920
Let's skip ahead by four bits now.

236
00:18:09,920 --> 00:18:14,160
Again we perform the subtraction, but this time the remainder is greater than zero, so

237
00:18:14,160 --> 00:18:16,840
we do not need to restore.

238
00:18:16,840 --> 00:18:23,520
We simply shift in a one into the quotient and then move on.

239
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:27,400
Let's skip ahead to the last bit position.

240
00:18:27,400 --> 00:18:32,560
We perform the subtraction and then find that the remainder is positive, so again, no restoration

241
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:35,400
needs to be performed.

242
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:43,160
We simply need to shift in a one to the quotient.

243
00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:48,120
So now we've gone through all the bits of the dividend, but before we finish up, because

244
00:18:48,120 --> 00:18:52,880
we skipped the first subtraction, we now have to right-shift the left half of the remainder

245
00:18:52,880 --> 00:19:01,920
as a correction, and now the final result can be found in the remainder register.

246
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:06,080
Though straightforward, this implementation can be quite inefficient since it requires

247
00:19:06,080 --> 00:19:12,640
two ALU cycles per bit position, one to perform the subtraction to check if the divisor fits,

248
00:19:12,640 --> 00:19:17,000
and one to perform the addition if restore is needed.

249
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,360
We can improve on this by implementing non-restoring division.

250
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:23,360
To do this, we start off similar to before.

251
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:29,360
We first subtract the divisor from the remainder, but this time, if the remainder is negative,

252
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:31,960
we won't restore right away.

253
00:19:31,960 --> 00:19:36,160
The correct remainder at this current bit position i is supposed to be whatever is in

254
00:19:36,160 --> 00:19:40,980
the remainder register plus the divisor due to restoration.

255
00:19:40,980 --> 00:19:46,780
So that means that the correct remainder after the next position i plus one would be equal

256
00:19:46,780 --> 00:19:54,180
to whatever is in the remainder register plus the divisor shifted left by one minus the divisor.

257
00:19:54,180 --> 00:19:58,460
Expanding on this, we find that it simply equals whatever is in the remainder register

258
00:19:58,460 --> 00:20:01,620
right now plus the divisor.

259
00:20:01,620 --> 00:20:07,360
So this implies that at each bit position, if the remainder is less than zero, that instead

260
00:20:07,360 --> 00:20:16,240
of subtracting the divisor next time, we actually need to add it instead.

261
00:20:16,240 --> 00:20:17,900
So let's see how this works.

262
00:20:17,900 --> 00:20:23,080
The hardware organization is similar to before, and the registers are initialized in the same

263
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:25,560
way as before.

264
00:20:25,560 --> 00:20:30,000
Just like in the improved restoring division, we start off by left shifting in a zero into

265
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:39,320
the remainder register as shown.

266
00:20:39,320 --> 00:20:42,840
Then the first thing we do is subtract the divisor from the left half of the remainder

267
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:44,560
as usual.

268
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:50,680
We find that the resulting remainder is not positive, but instead of restoring now, we

269
00:20:50,680 --> 00:20:56,600
simply left shift in a zero into the quotient and set a flag that says at the next bit position,

270
00:20:56,600 --> 00:21:03,720
we will add the divisor instead of subtracting it.

271
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:05,780
And that's just what we do.

272
00:21:05,780 --> 00:21:09,280
We add the divisor into the remainder register.

273
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:13,360
Again we check if the resulting remainder is positive.

274
00:21:13,360 --> 00:21:19,040
It's still negative, so we left shift in another zero and specify that we will perform add

275
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:24,400
again on the next bit position.

276
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:28,500
The same thing happens for the next three bits, so we'll skip ahead.

277
00:21:28,500 --> 00:21:31,320
At this bit position, we add the divisor.

278
00:21:31,320 --> 00:21:34,840
This time, we find that the remainder is positive.

279
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:39,560
So we left shift in a one into the quotient and specify that the next operation will be

280
00:21:39,560 --> 00:21:46,520
a subtraction instead.

281
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:50,760
Let's skip ahead again to the last bit position.

282
00:21:50,760 --> 00:21:55,080
Here we need to perform an addition again, so we add the divisor into the left half

283
00:21:55,080 --> 00:21:57,160
of the remainder register.

284
00:21:57,160 --> 00:22:01,000
We find that the remainder is positive.

285
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:10,180
So we just left shift in a one into the quotient.

286
00:22:10,180 --> 00:22:13,240
And now we've reached the end of the dividend.

287
00:22:13,240 --> 00:22:19,040
But just like before, we need to right shift the left half of the remainder register once

288
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:21,280
just to correct the remainder.

289
00:22:21,280 --> 00:22:25,520
And we have finished.

290
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:30,520
Let's end this lecture with a quick example of 4-bit boost multiplication.

291
00:22:30,520 --> 00:22:35,760
Consider a multiplicand of negative 6 and a multiplier of negative 2.

292
00:22:35,760 --> 00:22:38,640
Compute the booth encoding of the multiplier.

293
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:42,480
Enlist the partial products of the boost multiplication.

294
00:22:42,480 --> 00:22:51,680
Please take some time now to work through it.

295
00:22:51,680 --> 00:22:57,400
The booth encoding for the multiplier is 0, 0 minus 0.

296
00:22:57,400 --> 00:23:02,200
So the first partial product is simply the multiplicand times 0.

297
00:23:02,200 --> 00:23:08,480
The next partial product shifts the multiplicand by 1 and multiplies it by negative 1.

298
00:23:08,480 --> 00:23:14,080
And the last two partial products are shown simply multiplying by 0.

299
00:23:14,080 --> 00:23:17,080
Adding these up yields the final product of 12.

